codingChallenge.solutionExample:
// QUERY A: ROUTE TO OLTP (production row-oriented database)
// Reasoning: this is a point lookup - fetching one specific row by
// primary key. Row-oriented storage excels at this: all columns for
// the specific row are stored adjacently and retrieved in one disk
// read. OLAP column storage would perform WORSE here since it would
// need to read across 15+ separate column files to assemble one row.

// QUERY B: ROUTE TO OLAP (column-oriented OLAP system)
// Reasoning: this is exactly the workload column storage is designed
// for - aggregate scan reading only 2 columns (created_date,
// total_amount) across potentially millions of rows. Row-oriented
// storage would read all columns for all matching rows. Additionally,
// running this on the production database risks interfering with real
// user transactions.

// QUERY C: ROUTE TO OLTP (production row-oriented database)
// Reasoning: while this is an aggregation, it's scoped to ONE
// specific user_id and will likely match only a handful of rows.
// An index on user_id makes this a fast index lookup on the production
// database; this query pattern doesn't benefit from OLAP column
// storage since it reads very few rows.

